estion/id #uuid "298fb927-fe85-5338-90a6-5c059cd8c976" :question/math-academy-id "q-16686" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=24$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot {0}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-24 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,-\\sqrt{6})$ and $(0,\\sqrt{6}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16686/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a57ec9ff-99fd-5a3d-9a89-e76ded9d01cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16686"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913/selection/5d6ddc0a357455838dd208c68bd7847cd2ce06696734c7728fae8cef0d3a73cb" :answer/id #uuid "4dae1693-2394-5780-87b9-4402f3f51de8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" :answer/id #uuid "47c3a58a-002d-5430-9363-3e1e169760dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913/selection/14777e58fb1ae0541b33830cd8393f39573c4264422f9566bff6d50fdd8a6958" :answer/id #uuid "f9220d75-96d1-5f7f-b09b-9add6ac53eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913/selection/22ea2d41ffbcf0ea51816bb7140f5292784ff505c3f874828e003c122472996e" :answer/id #uuid "b7cfb89a-9908-51e5-81f9-0b45ddfc9a25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913/selection/e76c91522f793c23efe7d6263f792dcdb647daa95bd3c25bbcf1f8066894b0e0" :answer/id #uuid "a1b96deb-57d7-5343-8d96-6e257e029543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f13d6378-50f8-5fa0-aba2-c25bf5f38485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-72913/selection/14777e58fb1ae0541b33830cd8393f39573c4264422f9566bff6d50fdd8a6958" :answer-field/choices ["q-72913/selection/5d6ddc0a357455838dd208c68bd7847cd2ce06696734c7728fae8cef0d3a73cb" "q-72913/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" "q-72913/selection/14777e58fb1ae0541b33830cd8393f39573c4264422f9566bff6d50fdd8a6958" "q-72913/selection/22ea2d41ffbcf0ea51816bb7140f5292784ff505c3f874828e003c122472996e" "q-72913/selection/e76c91522f793c23efe7d6263f792dcdb647daa95bd3c25bbcf1f8066894b0e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-72913" :question/id #uuid "3538d2b4-fd15-5d04-9456-067812f7d246" :question/math-academy-id "q-72913" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-20x+6(0)+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(5-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-72913/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-72913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-327143/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "b17114f5-9be2-5607-814f-99024f980f92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327143/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a42f2386-1e7d-5f17-afe5-e0ce3e45191b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327143/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-327143/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327143/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "d7b782f3-9ea3-5c24-a6a2-0a2c9a0898fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327143/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1b17dc1a-ebba-555d-99c1-4bf002eb6a57" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327143/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-327143/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327143" :question/id #uuid "f03e4188-c5d2-5839-bbb9-153479b41318" :question/math-academy-id "q-327143" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12x+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+4{y}^{2}-12(0)+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+8y+4 & =0 \\\\ 4\\,[{y}^{2}+2y]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y+1)^{2}-1]+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}-4+4 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/assets/efaa5f474c5f505f6a1ca98469a77f8d21e059adee99c60cb9a2a7cb87600b5f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-327143/field-1" "q-327143/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-327143"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-327144/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "03f6b800-3d2d-5cd1-8610-d2263e984304" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327144/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "2b119996-3191-5522-91a2-c71d28bb06f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327144/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0fe0ded-7e43-5e65-b341-d71c3d415f42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327144/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-327144/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-327144/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327144/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "9a716c79-7ab5-569d-b6fb-927b17e1ad00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327144/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "813994d5-8190-5933-85c0-72fdde86b7c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327144/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-327144/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327144" :question/id #uuid "83504bae-41cf-56e1-be34-868e51df56b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-327144" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40x+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+25{y}^{2}-40(0)+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25{y}^{2}+100y+100 & =0 \\\\ 25\\,[{y}^{2}+4y+4] & =0 \\\\ 25\\,(y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043117/assets/78be4ca7215ee8dbf05a442080bae752e4fadfbe76084d975338d00e1bb02c78.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-327144/field-1" "q-327144/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fea426db-a854-5efc-a0cd-7d52019a7cc6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-327144"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-327163/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "5bbeb7e8-df47-5da9-aef5-af4a04827c3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327163/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c83d2c8-bf0c-5e4e-bd41-055bd75e4425" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327163/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-327163/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327163/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "25510343-3e0c-5bfb-b89d-1e14db304ea9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-327163/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1498bc64-9732-5e53-bbf1-1118ad7043b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-327163/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-327163/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-327163" :question/id #uuid "f8df360b-3490-5bf6-928c-aebad4f9cfa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-327163" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse  $5{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-20x+16y+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-20x+16y+20 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4\\cdot {0}^{2}-20x+16\\cdot 0+20 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-20x+20 & =0 \\\\ 5\\,[{x}^{2}-4x]+20 & =0 \\\\ 5[(x-2)^{2}-4]+20 & =0 \\\\ 5(x-2)^{2}-20+20 & =0 \\\\ 5(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at  $(2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-327163/field-1" "q-3